376:Let $D:= V(P) \setminus (S_3^+ \cup S_3^- \cup V(M))$. Let $D',S_1'$ be half-sets such that both $|D'\cap D|$ and $|S_1'\cap S_1|$ are maximized. Then, by \eqref{eq:size of S_1}, \eqref{eq:size of V(P)}, and \Cref{cl:size of S_3}, we have $|D'\setminus D|\le 32\eta n$ and $|S_1'\setminus S_1|\le 2\eta n$. By \Cref{lem:KLS general}, we have $e_G(D', S_1')\ge \alpha n^2/3$, thus there is an edge from a vertex $v_t\in D$ to a vertex $v_s\in S_1$. As $v_t\in D\subseteq \overline{S_3^+\cup S_3^-\cup V(M)}$, we have that $v_t$ is not adjacent to $v_s$ on $P$ and $v_tv_{t+1},v_tv_{t-1} \notin M$. Thus, the edge $v_tv_s$ can be used to perform a P\'osa rotation on $v_0\ldots v_{s-1}v_\ell v_{\ell-1}\ldots,v_s$ that fixes $v_0$ and gives a good path. Thus if $t<s$ then $v_{t+1}\in S_2$, else $v_{t-1}\in S_2$. In both cases, we have that $v_t\in S_2^+\cup S_2^-\subseteq S_3^+\cup S_3^-$, which contradicts the fact that $v_t\in D$. This finishes the proof of \Cref{thm:robustdirac}.
377:\end{proof}
